  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Mahle shut them down for six. The bullpen didn't blink. One run was enough.",
    "what_this_means": "Tyler Mahle threw a shutout into the sixth inning, and that became the whole story. The offense scraped together three runs. Grant McCray's home run in the fourth broke a scoreless tie and gave them breathing room they never had to surrender. They left runners on in the first two innings, squandered what could have been an early lead, but the pitching was so clean it didn't matter. Hentges, Smith, and Miller combined for three perfect innings after Mahle exited. No one Milwaukee sent to the plate found anything to hit. This is what 22 games back looks like from inside the game: not dramatic, not dominant, just controlled. The inconsistency that has defined the season got quieter for one night because a starter made it unnecessary to manufacture anything late. They won because they didn't have to.",
    "what_to_watch": "Logan Henderson takes the ball for Milwaukee tonight at 6:45 PT, carrying a 3.05 ERA into the series finale. Landen Roupp has worked to a 3.49 mark over his last four starts and will need to keep that momentum if the Giants want to finish the series even.",
